---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import PcWidthStrip from '../../components/pixelcheck/PcWidthStrip.astro';
import PcScoreBreakdown from '../../components/pixelcheck/PcScoreBreakdown.astro';
import PcStack from '../../components/pixelcheck/PcStack.astro';

const LIVE_URL = 'https://pixel-check-peach.vercel.app/';
const REPLAY_URL = 'https://pixel-check-peach.vercel.app/check/lovable-dashboard-check/replay';

const metrics = [
  { value: '38', label: 'Phone match score, while all 8 widths fit' },
  { value: '7', label: 'Texts my own shipped app lost on phones' },
  { value: '~1 min', label: 'Per check, about 5 cents' },
];

const tools = [
  'Nebius Token Factory',
  'NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Super',
  'Gemma 3 27B',
  'Playwright',
  'Python + FastAPI',
  'React + TypeScript',
  'Tailwind CSS',
  'Claude Code',
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="PixelCheck"
  subtitle="Holding AI-built UI to the designer's frames, at every screen size."
  description="PixelCheck measures any build against the designer's phone, tablet and desktop frames, explains why each size scored what it did, and writes a fix brief in which every line is checked against a measurement."
  heroImage="/images/pixelcheck/hero.png"
  ogImage="/images/og/pixelcheck.jpg"
  industry="AI Tooling / Design QA"
  role="Designer & builder (with Claude Code)"
  timeline="Sep – Oct 2026"
  platforms="Web"
  tools={tools}
  metrics={metrics}
  prevProject={{ title: 'Immunization', href: '/work/immunization' }}
  nextProject={{
    title: 'EqualTales',
    href: '/work/equaltales',
    subtitle: "AI-powered children's storybook",
    image: '/images/equaltales/hero.png'
  }}
>
  <!-- Try it -->
  <section class="case-section pc-try">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">Try it</p>
    <div class="pc-ctas">
      <a class="pc-cta pc-cta--primary" href={REPLAY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        See a real check<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pc-cta" href={LIVE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Open the live app<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="pc-note">
      The real check is a recorded run and opens instantly. Live checks run on a free server that can take up to
      a minute to wake.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">01 · The gap</p>
    <h2>It fits. It doesn't match.</h2>
    <p class="lead-text">
      AI builders like Lovable, v0 and Bolt turn a design into a working page in minutes, and it usually looks right
      on the laptop you check it on. On a phone it quietly drifts: smaller type, tighter spacing, the wrong font, a
      platform badge sitting on a button.
    </p>
    <p>
      Designers already make phone, tablet and desktop frames. The checks we have ask whether a page breaks: does
      anything overflow or overlap? I wanted to ask a designer's question instead: is it still my design, at every
      size? PixelCheck treats the frames as the spec and measures the build against them.
    </p>
    <PcWidthStrip />
  </section>

  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">02 · A real test</p>
    <h2>My own design, built by Lovable, measured</h2>
    <p>
      I gave Lovable my three <a href="/work/fitstart">FitStart</a> dashboard frames and one prompt, and took its
      first output. It looked right on my laptop. PixelCheck scored it 38 on phone, 61 on tablet and 65 on desktop,
      while all 8 widths fit. On a phone, content sat 150 px higher than the design by the bottom of the screen,
      yet nothing overflowed, so an overflow check would pass it.
    </p>
    <figure class="pc-figure full-width">
      <img src="/images/pixelcheck/check-result.webp" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="PixelCheck's result for the Lovable build: a ring showing 38.5 at the worst size (mobile), bars for mobile 38.5, tablet 61.1 and desktop 64.5 against a target of 90, an every-width strip with all 8 widths green, and a fix brief." />
      <figcaption class="pc-fig">
        Fig. 2. The result page. The headline is the worst size, and the strip shows every width fitting.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      One example, not a ranking of tools. The point is that the drift is invisible until something measures it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">03 · Designing the result</p>
    <h2>A score is only useful if it says why</h2>
    <p>
      The result page is the product, so I designed it the way I would any dashboard: what does someone need to
      decide, and in what order?
    </p>
    <ul class="pc-points">
      <li>
        <strong>The worst size is the headline.</strong> An average of 38, 61 and 65 would hide the phone, which is
        the whole problem.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fits and matches are kept apart.</strong> The green and red strip answers "does it break?" The score
        answers "is it the design?" A note on the card says a page can fit everywhere and still not match.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Explain the score; don't tune it.</strong> The scorer lines up the whole screen once, so spacing that
        drifts down the page costs points on every row below. Rather than change the score after seeing a result, the
        page shows points lost per factor and the drift band by band.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verdicts in plain words.</strong> "Same layout, drifting" and "Text about 11% smaller" before any
        number a designer has to decode.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <PcScoreBreakdown />
    <figure class="pc-figure full-width">
      <img src="/images/pixelcheck/why-overlay.webp" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="The 'Why mobile scored 38.5' panel beside a side-by-side view of the design and the build, with red boxes marking text that moved and a drift reading from top to bottom of the screen." />
      <figcaption class="pc-fig">
        Fig. 3. Why the phone scored 38.5: points lost per factor, what differs, and the design beside the build.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">04 · A fix brief you can trust</p>
    <h2>The model writes; the measurements decide what stays</h2>
    <p>
      A score tells you something is wrong. A designer handing work back to an AI builder needs to say what to
      change. PixelCheck gives NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Super a list of numbered, measured facts, and it writes a short
      brief to paste into Lovable, v0 or Bolt. Every line has to cite a fact.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then a rule-based verifier, outside the model, checks every line: numbers and units against the cited fact,
      quoted text, which screen size the instruction names, and words that overclaim a cause. Anything the
      measurements don't back is dropped, and the page says what it dropped and why.
    </p>
    <p>
      I built the verifier because the first briefs looked plausible but weren't safe to paste. One said "move the
      badge"; another gave a mobile-only fix without naming a size.
    </p>
    <figure class="pc-figure full-width">
      <img src="/images/pixelcheck/fix-brief.webp" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="The 'What to do next' panel: a message to paste into an AI builder with three numbered instructions, each tagged with the fact it cites (F1, F2, F3), and a footer saying Nemotron 3 Super wrote it from 8 measured facts and a check outside the model dropped 1 claim." />
      <figcaption class="pc-fig">
        Fig. 4. Each instruction carries the fact it rests on. In this check, the verifier dropped one claim.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">05 · No designs? Audit</p>
    <h2>Pointing it at my own shipped apps</h2>
    <p>
      Not every page has frames to compare with, so PixelCheck also audits a URL on its own: whether it breaks at
      any width, whether a platform badge covers content, and what text a phone loses. I ran it on three of my own
      deployed apps: two from my <a href="/work/ai-lab">AI Lab</a>, and <a href="/work/equaltales">EqualTales</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class="pc-points">
      <li><strong>Interview Sage:</strong> 7 texts shown on larger screens are gone on phones, and the Replit badge covers part of the "Generate Questions" button.</li>
      <li><strong>HeadshotAI:</strong> the "Edit with Lovable" badge covers part of the upload button on phones.</li>
      <li><strong>EqualTales:</strong> nothing to fix.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="pc-figure full-width">
      <img src="/images/pixelcheck/audit.webp" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="An audit of Interview Sage with no designs: every width fits, a warning that the Replit badge covers part of the Generate Questions button on mobile, and a list of 7 texts hidden on mobile." />
      <figcaption class="pc-fig">Fig. 5. An audit needs only a URL. It has no match score, because there is no design to compare with.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      PixelCheck can also rebuild a page from its frames as one responsive React and Tailwind codebase, and score
      the rebuild with the same checks. Rebuilding the home page of <a href="/work/portfolio-website">this site</a>
      scored 87.4 at its worst size, with the site and the rebuild both set in Inter for a like-for-like comparison.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">06 · How it's built</p>
    <h2>Models read and write; code measures and decides</h2>
    <p>
      The split is deliberate. Language models do what they are good at: reading frames and turning facts into
      instructions. Everything that decides a score or a claim is measured and checked in code, inside an isolated
      sandbox.
    </p>
    <PcStack />
  </section>

  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">07 · Trying to fool my own scorer</p>
    <h2>If a build can game the score, the score means nothing</h2>
    <p>
      When PixelCheck rebuilds a page, a model writes code that my scorer then grades, and code written against a
      scorer finds its weak spots. So I attacked my own scorer first, with builds that copy text once per screen
      size, pin a layout to one exact width, or hide text to collect points, and hardened it over two rounds. A
      rebuild caught cheating scores zero.
    </p>
    <p>
      I haven't found another tool that checks one build against phone, tablet and desktop frames at once. I don't
      claim to be the first to check AI-built UI visually.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Closing -->
  <section class="case-section">
    <p class="pc-eyebrow">Honest limits</p>
    <div class="pc-limits">
      <ul>
        <li>Each frame is scored on its first screen; full-page scoring is not done yet.</li>
        <li>Text reading is Latin-script only, and fonts come from 16 open families.</li>
        <li>About a minute and about 5 cents is for the example, whose design reading is ready ahead of time. New frames take longer to read.</li>
        <li>The Lovable build is one real example, not a benchmark.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>What I learned</h3>
    <p>
      Measurements are what make a language model useful and safe here. Nemotron is good at turning numbered facts
      into instructions a builder can follow. A verifier outside the model is what makes those instructions
      trustworthy.
    </p>

    <h3>What's next</h3>
    <p>
      A command-line version, so an AI coding agent can check its own UI at every size before it says "done".
    </p>

    <div class="pc-ctas pc-ctas--end">
      <a class="pc-cta pc-cta--primary" href={REPLAY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        See a real check<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pc-cta" href={LIVE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Open the live app<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  :root {
    /* PixelCheck's own burnt orange */
    --color-accent: #E5480F; /* bars, borders and the h2 rule: 4.87:1 on #0D0D0D (non-text needs 3:1) */
    --color-accent-text: #FF6A2B; /* small text on dark: 6.8:1 on #0D0D0D (WCAG AA) */
  }

  /* The arrival curve uses the deeper orange so its white title stays readable (6:1) */
  :global(#page-transition) {
    --color-accent: #B23A0C;
  }

  /* Calmer rhythm, as on Philly Truce: more air between chapters, no divider lines */
  :global(.case-content) .case-section {
    padding-bottom: 0;
    margin-bottom: clamp(88px, 10vw, 128px);
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  :global(.case-content) .case-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3.2vw, 2.25rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  :global(.decision-title) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  :global(.case-content) .pc-eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font: 500 12px/1.4 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent-text);
  }

  :global(.case-content) .case-section a:not(.pc-cta) {
    color: var(--color-accent-text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* Calls to action */
  .pc-try {
    margin-bottom: clamp(56px, 7vw, 80px) !important;
  }

  .pc-ctas {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .pc-ctas--end {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .pc-cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 48px;
    padding: 0 22px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--color-fg);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 0.2);
    transition: background-color 0.2s var(--ease-out, ease), box-shadow 0.2s var(--ease-out, ease);
  }

  .pc-cta:hover {
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--color-accent-text);
  }

  .pc-cta--primary {
    background: #B23A0C; /* white label 6:1 */
    color: #fff;
    box-shadow: none;
  }

  .pc-cta--primary:hover {
    background: #9a320a;
    box-shadow: none;
  }

  .pc-cta:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent-text);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  :global(.case-content) .pc-note {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  /* Figures */
  .pc-figure {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
  }

  :global(.case-content) .pc-figure img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: none;
    margin: 0;
    border-radius: 12px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 0.08);
  }

  :global(.case-content) .pc-fig {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  :global(.case-content) .pc-points li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .pc-limits {
    padding: 20px 24px;
    border-radius: 16px;
    background: #141414;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 0.07);
  }

  :global(.case-content) .pc-limits ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }

  :global(.case-content) .pc-limits li {
    margin: 0 0 4px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
